.I device ...
.br
.B spaces space
.BR create | delete | rename | resize
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.I device ...
.br
.B spaces tier create
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.I device ...
.br
.B spaces disk
.BR add | set | retire | remove
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.I device ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B spaces
reads the metadata of Microsoft Storage Spaces pools created by Windows 11
(pool versions 28 and 29) from their member disks and exposes the virtual
disks ("spaces") as Linux block devices, read-only unless a space is
attached with
.BR \-\-rw .
The
.BR pool ,
.B space
and
.B disk
commands create and change pools as Windows 11 24H2 does; they write only
with
.BR \-\-yes .
Pool members are never written otherwise.
.PP
Supported: simple, two- and three-way mirror, single and dual parity spaces
(including the local reconstruction code of 11 and more columns), storage
tiers and mirror-accelerated parity, fixed and thin provisioning, several
spaces per pool, the per-space write-back cache, 512e and 4Kn member disks,
pools with missing, failing or retired disks as far as the redundancy
allows, and pools after a crash. Writing: simple, mirror and single parity
spaces of clean pools, fixed and thin (not dual parity, storage tiers,
degraded pools, or rows of thin spaces with other allocation units than
256 MiB that are not allocated yet). Management: creating pools (version
28) and simple, mirror and single parity spaces, fixed and thin; deleting,
renaming and growing spaces; adding, retiring, removing and replacing
disks; tier templates and tiered spaces (an SSD mirror over an HDD simple
or parity tier); repair, optimize, scrub and a health report in Windows'
terms. Not supported: creating dual parity spaces, writing tiered spaces,
new spaces in pools of version 29, pools created by other Windows releases.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Lists the Storage Spaces pool members found among the block devices, grouped
by pool, with the spaces of each pool (as root; without, it says how many
devices it could not open).
.TP
.B attach
Attaches the spaces of every complete pool (or of the selected pool/space).
Each space appears as
.BI /dev/mapper/ss\- pool \- space
and every partition inside it as
.BI /dev/mapper/ss\- pool \- space \-p N\fR;
when a space of another pool of the same name has that name already, the
first eight hex digits of the pool's GUID follow the space's name.
Every space is checked first, as
.B check
does, and only healthy spaces are attached without being asked (so also
by the udev rule and the attach unit). A degraded space (redundancy
reduced: missing disks, out-of-date copies) is attached with
.BR \-\-degraded ,
a suspect one (the pool lacks its quorum, copies of the cache or of the
partition table disagree, the log of a ReFS volume holds changes Windows
would replay, ...) with
.BR \-\-force ,
both read-only; a failed one (data lost, metadata not understood) not at
all. The report goes to
.BI /run/storage-spaces/reports/ guid .txt
and
.BR .json ;
for a refused space also to standard error: whole on a terminal,
otherwise a line for the space and one per failing check (the attach
unit's lines in the journal). Spaces already attached are left alone.
Exits with 1 when a space or pool was not attached, and when
.B \-\-pool
or
.B \-\-space
names none of those found (without them, finding no pool is no error).
Every device attach makes gets udev properties (SS_SPACE, SS_POOL,
SS_VERDICT, SS_FORCED, from
.IR /run/storage\-spaces/udev ):
the udev rule hides from udisks2 (UDISKS_IGNORE) the devices of a space
attached past its verdict, so that no desktop mounts them unasked, and the
ublk, nbd or loop device a space is served through with its kernel
partitions, which duplicate the space's own devices.
With
.B \-\-rw
the spaces are attached read-write, if they are healthy and their state
is fully understood (clean pool, a supported resiliency); otherwise the
reason is printed. Mirror spaces keep their dirty region log, single parity spaces are
written through their write-back cache and parity journal, as Windows does.
Thin spaces allocate rows as writes first reach them; discards give rows of
thin simple and mirror spaces back to the pool once they cover them whole.
Writes are durable once flushed or after
.BR detach .
Pools on image files work too: the server may write the member images
(its view of the system is otherwise read-only). Not with
.B \-\-degraded
or
.BR \-\-force .
.IP
The checks of each pool run in a child process of its own, all at once,
with a memory limit and a deadline
.RB ( SPACES_POOL_MEMORY ,
.BR SPACES_POOL_TIMEOUT );
a pool whose child reaches one fails alone (space.checks), and nothing of it
is attached. The devices of several pools may be given at once.
.B attach
and
.B detach
wait for another run of either (udev starts them as disks come) until the
time of the run is up
.RB ( SPACES_ATTACH_TIMEOUT ),
or 10 minutes when started by hand, naming the process that runs; then they
fail. The programs they run (dmsetup, losetup, nbd\-client, systemctl,
udevadm, journalctl) are stopped after 120 s, or when the run's time is up.
.TP
.B detach
Removes the devices of the given space (its name as Windows gives it, its
GUID or its device-mapper name) or
of all attached spaces, and stops their serving processes; a name that
matches no attached space fails, and one that matches attached spaces of
two pools is refused, naming them. A space whose devices are in use stays
attached, nothing of it removed, with what uses it named: a mount (in any
mount namespace), another device built on it (LVM, dm-crypt), a swap, a
process that holds it open, or else the kernel's count of its openers. A
step that fails (a device that stays, a server that does not stop) keeps
the state with what is left, for the next detach.
.B \-\-outdated
detaches only the spaces whose serving process runs an older copy of
.B spaces
(replaced by an upgrade since it started), attached read-only as healthy
and not in use, names the others, and attaches the ones it detached again
as they were attached (the arguments their state records: devices or
image files, backend, flags); the packages run it after an upgrade, so
that the new version serves them.
.B \-\-leftovers
removes instead the device-mapper devices with the UUID of a space
.RB ( STORAGESPACES\- )
that no state lists; those in use stay. Both
.B attach
and
.B detach
first undo an attach that was cut off (its state left attaching), or
complete it where its devices are all there and in use.
.TP
.B status
Lists attached spaces, their backends, partition devices and verdicts
(with the report of those attached past it), attaches that were cut off,
and the spaces checked and not attached with the first line of their
reports (also for users other than root; not those whose devices are all
gone since); for root also the
device-mapper devices with the UUID of a space and no state.
.TP
.B check
Checks the spaces of every pool found (or of the pools whose devices are
given: the devices of several pools are checked each pool on its own) as
.B attach
does before it reads them, and prints a report per space: every check
(pool.quorum, pool.members, pool.database, pool.clean, space.state,
space.layout, space.cache, space.journal, space.drl, space.partitions,
fs.ntfs, fs.refs.boot, fs.refs.superblock, fs.refs.checkpoint,
fs.refs.log, fs for a partition of neither file system; pool.open for
disks that do not assemble into a pool, space.checks when the checks did
not finish; with
.BR \-\-deep ,
deep.scrub and deep.refs) with its status (ok, info, warning, skipped, degraded,
suspect, failed), its evidence (where: device and byte offset, LBA, and
for a byte of the space the slab, disk and physical offset that hold it;
what was expected and what was found, bytes in hex), where the truth
comes from (Windows' metadata, a cross-check, the format) and what to do.
A space's verdict is its worst status: healthy, degraded, suspect or
failed.
.B \-\-json
prints the reports as JSON with stable check ids and statuses;
.B \-\-deep
also compares every copy (deep.scrub, as
.B pool scrub
does), checks ReFS volumes whole (deep.refs, as
.B refs check
does) and every stripe the parity journal does not record as consistent
(otherwise up to 1 GiB is read); a scrub that cannot run makes the space
suspect;
.B \-\-bundle
.I file
also writes a .tar.gz with the reports,
.BR "spaces dump" ,
.B spaces extents
and the first and last 64 KiB of each space (its partition table) for a
bug report: no file data, but the names and GUIDs of pools, spaces and
disks (review it before sharing it). Exits with 1 unless every space is
healthy, and when there was nothing to check: no pool found (or none that
could be read), or no space that
.B \-\-space
names
.RB ( \-\-json
then prints an empty list).
.TP
.B serve\-ublk\fR, \fBserve\-nbd\fR, \fBserve\-fuse
Serve one space in the foreground, as
.B attach
starts them (as transient units); the same checks gate them
.RB ( \-\-degraded ,
.B \-\-force
as for attach), and
.B \-\-rw
serves it read-write (ublk and nbd).
.B serve\-ublk
makes
.BR /dev/ublkb N
(root, the
.B ublk_drv
module);
.B serve\-nbd \-\-socket
.I path
listens on a Unix socket only its owner may connect to, from the moment it
exists, for
.B nbd\-client \-unix
.IR path ;
.B serve\-fuse \-\-mountpoint
.I dir
exposes the space read-only as the file
.IR dir /space.img.
.B \-\-ready\-file
.I path
writes, once it serves, the device, the socket or the image file there,
whole. SIGINT and SIGTERM make what was written durable and end it.
.TP
.B info\fR, \fBextents\fR, \fBexport\fR, \fBdm\-table
Inspect a pool given by its member devices or images: pool, disks (usage,
media type, missing) and spaces (layout, provisioning, the verdict of the
checks, and whether they are healthy, degraded or failed with the disks at
hand, plus the state of the parity journal, dirty region tracking and
write-back cache); the slab allocation of a space; copy a space into a
new sparse image file, readable by its owner only, that appears whole or
not at all (as
.B attach
reads spaces:
.B \-\-degraded
and
.B \-\-force
for spaces that are not healthy); print a device-mapper table for a simple
or mirror space.
.TP
.B dump\fR, \fBdiff
Print the pool metadata one fact per line (every copy of the pool
database, its records raw and decoded, the dirty region log, parity journal
and write-back cache of each space), or the lines only one of two states of
a pool has, for comparing a pool before and after a change.
.SH MANAGEMENT
Every
.BR pool ,
.B space
and
.B disk
command takes the pool's disks (block devices or image files), prints the
plan of what it would write and writes it only with
.BR \-\-yes ,
in steps that each end with a flush, so that a crash leaves a pool that
opens in the state before or after a step. The disks are opened
exclusively (O_EXCL on block devices and a lock that every
.B spaces
process serving or writing them takes, for a partition on its whole disk
too): detach the pool first; the
refusal names what holds them. Only
clean pools are changed (every disk present, no out-of-date copy, every
record understood), except that
.BR "pool repair" ,
.B disk add
and
.B disk remove
accept missing disks, to replace them. Disks are named by their ids as
.B info
lists them.
.TP
.B pool create \-\-name \fIname\fR [\-\-logical\-sector 512|4096] [\-\-wipe]
A pool of the given disks, which must be blank (nothing in their first and
last MiB) unless
.B \-\-wipe
is given, which destroys what they hold.
.TP
.B pool rename \-\-name \fIname\fR, \fBpool remove
Rename the pool; remove a pool without spaces (its partitions leave the
disks' partition tables).
.TP
.B pool repair
Rebuild every copy on a missing disk or out of date on another disk, from a
current mirror copy or the other parity columns.
.TP
.B pool optimize
Spread the extents evenly over the disks, as after adding one.
.TP
.B pool scrub [\-\-repair]
Compare every copy of the mirror spaces and every stripe of the single
parity spaces, and of such tiers of tiered spaces; differences where writes
were under way are counted apart.
.B \-\-repair
makes everything agree (the first mirror copy, parity from the data).
.TP
.B pool health
The HealthStatus and OperationalStatus Windows would show for the pool, its
disks and spaces with the disks given, and the disk failures each space
still survives.
.TP
.B space create \-\-name \fIname\fR \-\-resiliency simple|mirror|parity \-\-size \fIsize\fR
With
.BR \-\-thin ,
.BI \-\-copies " 2|3" ,
.BI \-\-columns " n" ,
.BI \-\-interleave " size" ,
.BI \-\-write\-cache " size"
(parity); Windows' defaults otherwise. Sizes such as 10G are rounded up to
whole rows.
.TP
.B space create \-\-name \fIname\fR \-\-tier \fItemplate\fB=\fIsize\fR \-\-tier \fItemplate\fB=\fIsize
A tiered space: an SSD tier, then an HDD tier, each from a template, with
its cache and logs on the SSD disks.
.TP
.B tier create \-\-name \fIname\fR \-\-media ssd|hdd \-\-resiliency simple|mirror|parity [\-\-columns \fIn\fR]
A tier template (New-StorageTier); the disks' media come from
.BR "disk set \-\-media" .
.TP
.B space delete\fR|\fBrename\fR|\fBresize \-\-space \fIname\fR
Delete a space and its data, rename it
.RB ( \-\-name ),
or grow it
.RB ( \-\-size ;
the file system inside is not grown).
.TP
.B disk add \-\-new \fIdevice\fR [\-\-wipe]
Add a blank disk to the pool of the other devices (at most four). A disk
with a partition table, a file system or pool data in its first or last MiB
is refused, unless
.BR \-\-wipe ,
which clears its first 17 MiB and last MiB first, as
.B pool create \-\-wipe
does.
.TP
.B disk set \-\-disk \fIid\fR [\-\-media unspecified|hdd|ssd] [\-\-usage auto\-select|manual\-select|hot\-spare]
Change a disk's media type or usage.
.TP
.B disk retire \-\-disk \fIid\fR, \fBdisk remove \-\-disk \fIid
Move everything off a disk; remove a disk that holds nothing any more, or a
missing one after a repair.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-space " " \fIkey\fR
A space by its GUID, its numeric id (as
.B spaces info
lists them) or its name, for every command. A key that names several
spaces of a pool (a name two spaces share, or a name that is another
space's GUID or id) is refused, naming them;
.B space create
and
.B space rename
refuse a new name that is the name, GUID or id of a space already there.
.TP
.BR \-\-pool " " \fIkey\fR
A pool by its GUID or its name. A name two pools share, or a name that is
another pool's GUID, is refused, naming them by their GUIDs.
.TP
.BR \-\-unclean\-parity " " refuse | data
After an unclean shutdown (crash, power loss, disks pulled), parity
stripes, mirror rows and write-back cache slots with writes in flight can
be inconsistent. Windows repairs parity stripes when it mounts the pool,
and which side it keeps is not recorded; mirror copies it never reconciles
and reads either one. By default reads of parity stripes whose parity does
not match, of mirror rows whose copies differ and of write-back cache
chunks whose mirrored copies disagree fail with an I/O error;
.B data
returns the data as it is on disk (the highest mirror copy, the newest
cache and journal entries). Writers read so too (the rest of a stripe they
write whole). Attaching the pool to Windows once repairs its parity
stripes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SPACES_CHECK_TIMEOUT
Seconds the checks of one space, and the opening of one pool, may take in
.BR attach ,
.B check
and
.B info
(40 by default, 0 for no limit;
.B check \-\-deep
has none). Checks that take longer make the space suspect (space.checks);
checks that stop on an internal error fail it. Either way the other pools
and spaces are checked and attached as usual.
.TP
.B SPACES_POOL_MEMORY
MiB of address space the checks of one pool may take: each pool is checked
in a child process of its own (2048 by default, 0 for no limit). A pool
whose checks need more fails (space.checks), and nothing of it is attached.
.TP
.B SPACES_POOL_TIMEOUT
Seconds the child that checks one pool may take (120 by default, 0 for no
limit; never past half of what is left of
.BR SPACES_ATTACH_TIMEOUT ,
so that the other pools have the rest to be attached in;
.B check \-\-deep
none unless this sets one).
A child that takes longer is killed, and its pool fails (space.checks).
.TP
.B SPACES_ATTACH_TIMEOUT
Seconds a run of
.B attach
may take (no limit by default; the attach units set 80, within their start
timeout): every wait, for udev, a server, another run's lock or the checks,
ends by then, and the spaces it does not get to are named and not attached.
Without it a run waits 10 minutes at most for another one's lock.
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B dm
Device-mapper table over the member devices; no process. Used by
.B auto
for simple spaces whose logical sector size matches the members' (read-write
only when the space is fully allocated and its write-back cache is empty).
.TP
.B ublk
A
.B spaces serve\-ublk
process (transient systemd unit) behind
.BR /dev/ublkb N;
needs Linux 6.0 or newer and the
.B ublk_drv
module. Exposes the space's own sector size and keeps reading when a mirror
copy or parity column fails. A server that stops on an internal error
fails the requests in flight, removes its device and ends with an error;
.B detach
deletes the device of a server that was killed before it could. A signal
that comes before the device starts ends the server without serving.
.TP
.B nbd
A
.B spaces serve\-nbd
process connected with
.BR nbd\-client (8).
When it is out of file descriptors it goes on accepting connections once
some close, and a listener that fails for good ends it with an error.
.TP
.B fuse
A
.B spaces serve\-fuse
process exposing an image file, attached with
.BR losetup (8).
Read-only. SIGINT and SIGTERM unmount it (lazily while the image file is
open) before it ends; a mount a killed server left is removed by the next
attach.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/storage-spaces/
State of attached spaces.
.TP
.I /run/storage-spaces/reports/
The reports of the checks of attach and
.B refs mount
(as root), a text and a JSON file per space or volume.
.TP
.I /run/storage-spaces/udev/
The udev properties of the devices attach made.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/69-storage-spaces.rules
.TQ
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/69-storage-spaces.rules
Starts
.BI storage-spaces-attach@ major : minor .service
when a pool member appears, a run of its own for each;
.B storage-spaces-attach.service
attaches at boot. The packages install the first rule,
.B contrib/install.sh
the second. To attach by hand only, remove the second, or override the
first with
.B ln \-s /dev/null /etc/udev/rules.d/69-storage-spaces.rules
(or
.BR "systemctl mask storage-spaces-attach.service" ,
which the runs of the rule heed too).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
spaces scan
spaces attach
mount \-o ro /dev/mapper/ss\-Storage_pool\-Data\-p2 /mnt
spaces detach
spaces attach \-\-rw \-\-space Data
.PP
spaces pool create \-\-name Data \-\-yes /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd
spaces space create \-\-name Files \-\-resiliency mirror \-\-size 1T \\
    \-\-yes /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd
spaces pool health /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd
spaces disk add \-\-new /dev/sde \-\-yes /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
